),e3(t1)]\nt2: e2 success. append. mem=[e0(t0),e3(t1),e2(t2)] (size 3)\nt3: e1 failure. Remove all entries with signature 'e1' -> none. len=3 -> need evict. Failures? none, only successes. Oldest success is e0(t0). Remove e0, then append e1 failure (t3). mem=[e3(t1),e2(t2),e1(t3)]\nt4: e1 success. Not failure, no removal. len=3, need evict. Now failures present? Yes, e1 is failure. So we evict a success first: successes are e3(t1) and e2(t2). Oldest success is e3(t1). Remove e3. Then append e1 success (t4). mem=[e2(t2),e1(t3,failure),e1(t4,success)] - note two e1.\nt5: e1 failure. Remove all entries with signature 'e1' -> remove both e1(t3) and e1(t4). mem becomes [e2(t2)] (size1). Then len=1<3, append e1 failure (t5). mem=[e2(t2),e1(t5,failure)]\nt6: e1 failure. Remove all entries with signature 'e1' -> remove e1(t5). mem=[e2(t2)] (size1). Append e1 failure(t6). mem=[e2(t2),e1(t6,failure)]\nAfter all, sort by last_trial: both in order already (t2=2, t6=6). memory = [ {last_trial:2, signature:'e2', reflection:'r2', success:true}, {last_trial:6, signature:'e1', reflection:'r6', success:false} ]\nsignatures = ['e2','e1'].\nIs this plausible? Possibly.\n\nNow case b-001:\nTrials:\n0: e3,sTrue\n1: e2,sFalse\n2: e1,sFalse\n3: e0,sFalse\n4: e2,sFalse\n5: e1,sFalse\n6: e3,sTrue\n\nSimulate:\ncapacity=3\nmem=[]\n\nt0: e3 success, no removal. mem=[e3(t0,true)]\nt1: e2 failure. Remove e2 (none). len=1<3, append. mem=[e3(t0),e2(t1,false)]\nt2: e1 failure. Remove e1 (none). len=2<3, append. mem=[e3(t0),e2(t1),e1(t2,false)] size=3\nt3: e0 failure. No removal (e0 not present). len=3, need evict. Failures present: e2 and e1 are failures. So evict a success first: e3 is success. Remove e3 (oldest success). Append e0 failure. mem=[e2(t1),e1(t2),e0(t3,false)]\nt4: e2 failure. Remove all entries with signature e2 -> remove e2(t1). mem=[e1(t2),e0(t3)] size=2. Then len=2<3, append e2 failure(t4). mem=[e1(t2),e0(t3),e2(t4,false)]\nt5: e1 failure. Remove all e1 -> remove e1(t2). mem=[e0(t3),e2(t4)] size=2. Append e1 failure(t5). mem=[e0(t3),e2(t4),e1(t5,false)]\nt6: e3 success. Not failure, no removal. len=3, need evict. Failures present: e0,e2,e1 all false. No successes. So evict oldest failure: oldest last_trial among failures is e0(t3). Remove e0. Append e3 success(t6). mem=[e2(t4),e1(t5),e3(t6,true)]\nSort by last_trial: order t4,t5,t6 -> e2(t4), e1(t5), e3(t6). memory = [ {last_trial:4,sig:'e2',refl:'r4',success:false}, {last_trial:5,sig:'e1',refl:'r5',success:false}, {last_trial:6,sig:'e3',refl:'r6',success:true} ]\nsignatures = ['e2','e1','e3'].\n\nCase b-002:\nTrials:\n0: e2,sFalse\n1: e1,sTrue\n2: e0,sTrue\n3: e1,sFalse\n4: e3,sFalse\n5: e3,sFalse\n6: e2,sFalse\n\nSimulate:\nt0: e2 failure. Remove none. mem=[e2(t0,false)]\nt1: e1 success. no removal. mem=[e2(t0), e1(t1,true)]\nt2: e0 success. no removal. mem=[e2(t0),e1(t1),e0(t2,true)] size=3\nt3: e1 failure. Remove all e1 -> remove e1(t1). mem=[e2(t0),e0(t2)] size=2. Append e1 failure(t3). mem=[e2(t0),e0(t2),e1(t3,false)]\nt4: e3 failure. Remove none. len=3, need evict. Failures present: e2 and e1 are failures. So evict a success first: e0 is success. Remove e0 (oldest success? it's the only success). Append e3 failure(t4). mem=[e2(t0),e1(t3),e3(t4,false)]\nt5: e3 failure. Remove all e3 -> remove e3(t4). mem=[e2(t0),e1(t3)] size=2. Append e3 failure(t5). mem=[e2(t0),e1(t3),e3(t5,false)]\nt6: e2 failure. Remove all e2 -> remove e2(t0). mem=[e1(t3),e3(t5)] size=2. Append e2 failure(t6). mem=[e1(t3),e3(t5),e2(t6,false)]\nSort by last_trial: t3=3, t5=5, t6=6 -> e1(t3), e3(t5), e2(t6). memory = [ {last_trial:3,sig:'e1',refl:'r3',success:false}, {last_trial:5,sig:'e3',refl:'r5',success:false}, {last_trial:6,sig:'e2',refl:'r6',success:false} ]\nsignatures = ['e1','e3','e2'].\n\nThese simulations seem plausible. However, note that in case b-001, after t6, memory contains e2,e1,e3 with last_trial 4,5,6. The output schema requires signatures array. Could be that order is by last_trial ascending.\n\nWe need to ensure we treat \"failure signature\" deduplication only for failures, but also the description \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" It might also mean that if a new success occurs with same signature as an existing failure, we do not deduplicate (since it's not a failure). That seems consistent.\n\nAlso during eviction, \"prioritize failures before successes\" means we prefer to keep failures, so we evict successes first. Recency then signature: among candidates, choose the one with the smallest last_trial (oldest). If same recency (impossible), then by signature.\n\nWe also need to \"Restore chronological order in the final memory.\" So sort by last_trial.\n\nNow, implement solve(case) that takes payload dict with keys 'capacity' and 'trials'. trial has 'signature', 'reflection', 'success'. We'll iterate using enumerate to assign last_trial index (0-based). Return dict with 'memory' and 'signatures'.\n\nEdge cases: capacity may be 0? Possibly but not shown. We'll handle defensively.\n\nWe must not mutate case. Use standard library only.\n\nNow produce the Python source as a JSON string.\n\nWe need to output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\", value is a string of Python code. Use strict JSON escaping.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    capacity = case['capacity']\n    trials = case['trials']\n    memory = []  # list of dicts with last_trial, reflection, signature, success\n    for idx, trial in enumerate(trials):\n        # create candidate entry\n        entry = {\n            'last_trial': idx,\n            'reflection': trial['reflection'],\n            'signature': trial['signature'],\n            'success': trial['success']\n        }\n        # if this trial is a failure, deduplicate: remove all existing entries with same signature\n        if not trial['success']:  # failure\n            memory = [m for m in memory if m['signature'] != trial['signature']]\n        # now try to add entry, possibly evicting\n        if len(memory) < capacity:\n            memory.append(entry)\n        else:\n            # need to evict one\n            # find candidates for eviction: separate successes and failures\n            successes = [(i, m) for i, m in enumerate(memory) if m['success']]\n            failures = [(i, m) for i, m in enumerate(memory) if not m['success']]\n            if successes:\n                # evict the oldest success (smallest last_trial)\n                oldest_success_idx = min(range(len(successes)), key=lambda k: successes[k][1]['last_trial'])\n                idx_to_remove = successes[oldest_success_idx][0]\n            else:\n                # evict the oldest failure\n                oldest_failure_idx = min(range(len(failures)), key=lambda k: failures[k][1]['last_trial'])\n                idx_to_remove = failures[oldest_failure_idx][0]\n            # remove that entry\n            memory.pop(idx_to_remove)\n            memory.append(entry)\n    # final sort by last_trial\n    memory.sort(key=lambda x: x['last_trial'])\n    # build output\n    output = {\n        'memory': memory,\n        'signatures': [m['signature'] for m in memory]\n    }\n    return output\n```\n\nBut note: after deduplication and before eviction, we compare len(memory) < capacity. However, if we had multiple entries with same signature (if dedup only for failures, successes could accumulate duplicates), but after dedup we might have removed some, so len could be less. This is fine.\n\nPotential issue: When we evict, we might have multiple successes with same last_trial? Not possible because last_trial is unique index.\n\nAlso, ensure we handle case where after dedup, len(memory) could be >= capacity? That's unlikely because we only removed entries, so len decreases. But if dedup removed zero, we might be at capacity exactly. So condition len(memory) < capacity works.\n\nHowever, there's a nuance: when we add a failure trial, we first remove all existing with same signature, then we add. If capacity is e.g., 3 and after removal we have 3 entries, then len(memory) == capacity, so we will go to else branch and evict one before adding. But we want to add the new entry after eviction. That's correct because we have already removed the old ones, and the new one should be added, potentially causing an eviction.\n\nBut what if after removal, len(memory) < capacity, we add without eviction. That seems right.\n\nOne more thing: The problem says \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" Our implementation for failure trials removes all existing entries with that signature and then adds the new one, which effectively retains the newest reflection. For success trials, we do not deduplicate, so multiple entries for same signature can exist; but later when a failure with that signature comes, it will remove all (including successes) and add the new reflection. That seems to retain the newest reflection for that failure signature.\n\nNow, about signatures array: we include all signatures from memory in order of last_trial.\n\nThis should produce results as per our simulation.\n\nNow, we need to output the code as a JSON string inside a single object. Let's format.\n\nI'll write:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    capacity = case['capacity']\\n    trials = case['trials']\\n    memory = []\\n    for idx, trial in enumerate(trials):\\n        entry = {\\n            'last_trial': idx,\\n            'reflection': trial['reflection'],\\n            'signature': trial['signature'],\\n            'success': trial['success']\\n        }\\n        if not trial['success']:\\n            memory = [m for m in memory if m['signature'] != trial['signature']]\\n        if len(memory) < capacity:\\n            memory.append(entry)\\n        else:\\n            successes = [(i, m) for i, m in enumerate(memory) if m['success']]\\n            if successes:\\n                idx_to_remove = min(successes, key=lambda x: x[1]['last_trial'])[0]\\n            else:\\n                failures = [(i, m) for i, m in enumerate(memory) if not m['success']]\\n                idx_to_remove = min(failures, key=lambda x: x[1]['last_trial'])[0]\\n            memory.pop(idx_to_remove)\\n            memory.append(entry)\\n    memory.sort(key=lambda x: x['last_trial'])\\n    return {'memory': memory, 'signatures': [m['signature'] for m in memory]}\"\n}\n```\n\nBut we need to ensure proper escaping of newlines and quotes inside the JSON string. I'll write a compact version with escaped newlines. Actually, we can embed newline characters as \\n. Also need to escape backslashes and quotes. I'll output properly.\n\nBetter to produce the JSON with Python's json.dumps? But we are writing the response directly. I'll manually escape.\n\nLet's produce the string.\n\nNote: The code uses single quotes for dict keys, which is fine. The JSON string value should have double quotes around the entire source. Inside the source, we have single quotes and double quotes? We only use single quotes, so no need to escape double quotes. But we need to escape backslashes (none) and newlines. So we replace each newline with \\n.\n\nI'll write the code with explicit \\n.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a multiline string with triple quotes? But JSON string must be on one line in the value. So we'll concatenate with \\n.\n\nLet's craft:\n\n\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    capacity = case['capacity']\\n    trials = case['trials']\\n    memory = []\\n    for idx, trial in enumerate(trials):\\n        entry = {\\n            'last_trial': idx,\\n            'reflection': trial['reflection'],\\n            'signature': trial['signature'],\\n            'success': trial['success']\\n        }\\n        if not trial['success']:\\n            memory = [m for m in memory if m['signature'] != trial['signature']]\\n        if len(memory) < capacity:\\n            memory.append(entry)\\n        else:\\n            successes = [(i, m) for i, m in enumerate(memory) if m['success']]\\n            if successes:\\n                idx_to_remove = min(successes, key=lambda x: x[1]['last_trial'])[0]\\n            else:\\n                failures = [(i, m) for i, m in enumerate(memory) if not m['success']]\\n                idx_to_remove = min(failures, key=lambda x: x[1]['last_trial'])[0]\\n            memory.pop(idx_to_remove)\\n            memory.append(entry)\\n    memory.sort(key=lambda x: x['last_trial'])\\n    return {'memory': memory, 'signatures': [m['signature'] for m in memory]}\"\n\nBut note: the line with 'successes = ...' uses lambda, which is fine.\n\nAlso need to ensure that 'memory.sort' is done.\n\nWe also must not mutate case. Not mutating.\n\nNow, produce the final JSON with the single key \"implementation\".\n\nLet's output it."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1611,"completion_tokens":7405,"total_tokens":9016,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":7082},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":75},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}